+//! Tether: a TURN server (RFC 5766, UDP) inside hbbs, on hbbs's own UDP port (21116), so a
+//! WebRTC session that cannot form a direct pair is relayed over UDP instead of hbbr's
+//! TCP/WebSocket leg. The core's WebRTC data channel (KCP-style SCTP, see
+//! vendor/rustdesk/Cargo.toml) recovers a loss within about an RTT, where TCP holds every later
+//! byte back (head-of-line blocking) and shrinks its window.
+//!
+//! Single port: every allocation's relayed address is `<public IP>:21116`, the port the relays
+//! already open. hbbs hands each datagram to `Turn::dispatch` first: TURN requests and the
+//! ChannelData of known TURN clients go to the TURN server, datagrams from a peer an allocation
+//! has sent to go to that allocation, everything else (rendezvous, STUN binding) stays with hbbs.
+//! Peers are told apart by their address, which is what a relayed address normally does by port.
+//!
+//! Credentials follow the TURN REST API (draft-uberti-behave-turn-rest): username
+//! `<unix expiry>:<tag>`, password `base64(HMAC-SHA1(secret, username))`. The secret is random per
+//! hbbs process and never leaves it: hbbs issues a credential for itself in `PunchHole.turn_server`
+//! (over the host's TLS WebSocket) for each WebRTC offer. Clients carry no TURN secret.
+//!
+//! Abuse limits (a TURN server sends wherever it is told): no relaying to or from private,
+//! loopback, link-local, CGNAT, multicast or reserved addresses, IPv4-mapped/embedded forms of
+//! those, or the relay's own public address; at most `TURN_MAX_ALLOCATIONS` allocations,
+//! `TURN_MAX_ALLOCATIONS_PER_IP` of them per client IP, and `MAX_PEERS` peers per allocation; a per-allocation bandwidth cap; a credential opens new
+//! allocations only for `CREDENTIAL_TTL`.
+
